entional weight loss", "Neurological symptoms (trouble focusing)", "Hypercalcemia with normal PTH (implied by unmentioned PTH levels and lab context)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Primary Hyperparathyroidism", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.036, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Normal PTH levels and antacid overuse (GERD history) strongly support milk-alkali syndrome as a non-PTH/malignancy cause of hypercalcemia, though no direct LR …"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Malignancy-associated Hypercalcemia", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of hypercalciuria and tumor markers in the lab profile weakly argues against malignancy-associated hypercalcemia, though subacute neurological symptoms co…"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Vitamin-D/Granulomatous Hypercalcemia", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.5740274038487829, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Elevated alkaline phosphatase (ALP) without hepatic dysfunction is a strong indicator of granulomatous hypercalcemia (e.g., sarcoidosis) due to its bone and ex…"], "evidence_against": ["Low urine calcium weakens the granulomatous hypercalcemia hypothesis (e.g., sarcoidosis) which typically presents with hypercalciuria, but this does not rule o…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B3.1", "B3.2", "B3.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.85, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["primary hyperparathyroidism", "humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "milk-alkali syndrome", "sarcoidosis"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Other Non-PTH/Malignancy Hypercalcemia Causes", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.49061083768095753, "posterior": 0.39002503647352477, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Antacid overuse in the context of poorly controlled GERD provides moderate support for milk-alkali syndrome as a hypercalcemia mechanism, aligning with the 'Ot…"], "evidence_against": ["Low urine calcium contradicts milk-alkali syndrome, which typically exhibits hypercalciuria due to excessive calcium intake, but this finding is consistent wit…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.75, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["primary hyperparathyroidism", "humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "milk-alkali syndrome", "sarcoidosis"]}, "B3.1": {"id": "B3.1", "label": "Sarcoidosis-related Hypercalcemia", "parent": "B3", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.3826849358991886, "posterior": 0.3826849358991886, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.85, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": ["primary hyperparathyroidism", "humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "milk-alkali syndrome", "sarcoidosis"]}, "B3.2": {"id": "B3.2", "label": "Hypervitaminosis D-induced Hypercalcemia", "parent": "B3", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.12756164529972955, "posterior": 0.12756164529972955, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.