he elevated alkaline phosphatase (150 U/L) without evidence of liver dysfunction support granulomatous hypercalcemia (e.g., sarcoidosis) as the mechanism in this subacute presentation?", "summary": "Elevated alkaline phosphatase (ALP) without hepatic dysfunction is a strong indicator of granulomatous hypercalcemia (e.g., sarcoidosis) due to its bone and extracellular enzyme release, though this must be reconciled with contradictory urinary calcium findings."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the history of poorly controlled GERD and antacid use support milk-alkali syndrome as the most likely cause of hypercalcemia in this patient?", "summary": "Antacid overuse in the context of poorly controlled GERD provides moderate support for milk-alkali syndrome as a hypercalcemia mechanism, aligning with the 'Other Non-PTH/Malignancy' category."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the low urine calcium (5 mmol/24 hours) contradict granulomatous hypercalcemia (e.g., sarcoidosis) as the primary mechanism in this case?", "summary": "Low urine calcium weakens the granulomatous hypercalcemia hypothesis (e.g., sarcoidosis) which typically presents with hypercalciuria, but this does not rule out other non-PTH/malignancy causes."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the low urine calcium (5 mmol/24 hours) contradict milk-alkali syndrome as a cause of hypercalcemia in this patient?", "summary": "Low urine calcium contradicts milk-alkali syndrome, which typically exhibits hypercalciuria due to excessive calcium intake, but this finding is consistent with other non-PTH/malignancy mechanisms."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B3", 0.575], ["B4", 0.341], ["B1", 0.081]]}, {"top": [["B4", 0.491], ["B3", 0.464], ["B1", 0.045]]}, {"top": [["B3", 0.574], ["B4", 0.39], ["B3.1", 0.383]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No acute life-threatening instability markers present", "required_actions": []}, 