lternatives": ["B3.1", "B3.2", "B3.3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=20%, source=both]\n\nRelated to Primary Hyperparathyroidism: familial isolated hyperparathyroidism, hyperparathyroidism, hyperparathyroidism 2 with jaw tumors\n\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.52):]\n  Source: Hyperparathyroidism. > Differential Diagnosis\n  Malignancy-Related Hypercalcemia\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.56):]\n  Source: Hypercalcemia of Malignancy. > Evaluation\n  The initial evaluation of hypercalcemia requires a comprehensive history and physical examination, which can help identify the underlying cause and pathology. Prior laboratory data are valuable, provi\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.59):]\n  Source: Hyperparathyroidism. > Differential Diagnosis\n  Malignancy-Related Hypercalcemia\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.62):]\n  Source: Hypercalcemia of Malignancy. > Introduction\n  More than 90% of hypercalcemia cases are caused by primary hyperparathyroidism (PHPT) or malignancy-induced hypercalcemia, with malignancy being the most common cause of hypercalcemia in hospitalized \n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  cough for the past week → sarcoidosis → Sarcoidosis-related Hypercalcemia\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Investigate for sarcoidosis via chest imaging (e.g., CT) and check for hypercalcemia due to granulomatous activity\n  abdominal pain and unexplained weight loss → malignancy with hypercalcemia of malignancy → Other Non-PTH/Malignancy Hypercalcemia Causes\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Rule out malignancy with hypercalcemia through tumor markers, imaging (e.g., PET/CT), and serum calcium/PTH evaluation\n\nWhen indirect_reas